A||N||8||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Duke Questoins:  Can you port-match without doing a valve job?||||5||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-14-2001||06:07 PM||||I am replacing a bad head gasket on my 1984 2.5L 4cylinder automatic.  It's been interesting as the car seems to be a victim of mister "Bad Wrench."  The intake and exhaust manifold bolts were finger tight or less!  However, the head bolts were really tight.  The cause of failure seems to be no copper gasket sealant on the head gasket.  I still need to use a straight edge to check for warpage . . .<BR>Questions:<BR>1.  Can the intake and exhaust ports on the head be ported (cleaned up) without doing a vavle job?<BR>2.  Is brush applied copper gasket sealant the best choice for the head gasket?  How do you recommend it be applied?  I am thinking about applying it to the block, then the top side of the head gasket.<BR>3.  Can pushrods or lifters be replaced without removing the camshaft?<BR>4.  How can the camshaft be removed while the engine is still in the car?<P>My plan is to port/clean up the intake and exhaust manifolds and then reassemble the engine.  But since I have it dissassembled right now, I can't help but think about this stuff. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||01-14-2001||06:21 PM||||I never heard of copper gasket sealant being used on the head gasket. Your not supposed to use any sealant on the head gasket. <P><BR>1, Yes as long as you don’t touch the valves or any sealing serfaces. Use an old set of valves to protect the seats. <BR>2, Check the heads and the deck for straightness and install the head gasket dry. Use the proper torque sequence. You have to get new head blots also because the Iron Duke uses torque to yield bolts.<BR>3, I don’t know.<BR>4, Don’t port the intake of exhaust manifolds on this motor. You will lose your low end torque, which is all the Iron Duke has with a 4500 rpm redline. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-14-2001||11:46 PM||||You DO NOT coat the head gasket with sealant. If you use any sealant at all you put a THIN coat of either Permatex Super 300 or Balkamp Aviation paint on sealers just around the water jacket holes. With a good head gasket no sealer is needed.<P>Any head bolts that go into the water jacket also need to be painted with one of the above sealers before installing.<P>DO NOT reuse the head bolts. 4 cylinder head bolts are "toque to yeild" and MUST be replaced each time they are removed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FieroGTRacingInc||01-14-2001||11:56 PM||||theogre...  Are the head bolt's a one time use on them too??   Thanks<P>------------------<BR>1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rnoswal||01-15-2001||02:37 AM||||If you are going to take the head off, then get a valve job done. If the head has any miles on it then you could have worn guides, valves and valve seats. A good 3 angle valve job will flow better making more horsepower and better fuel mileage. There may also be a build up of carbon on the undersides of the valves that will need cleaning. You can also do a much better porting job, especially in the bowl area which needs it the most. It probably could use better valve springs too. This will save you in the long run.<P>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000005||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-15-2001||03:19 AM|||| The engine has a rebuilt head with about 5,000 miles on it.  There are 90K miles on the block now.  A compression test yielded pressures of 170, 120, 115, and 165 psi for each of the cylinders.  The valves are free of carbon. The pistons and bores are clean and free of carbon build up.  <BR>The head was replaced about 5K miles ago as the original cracked.  It was a professional job, but the first mechanic left in the middle of it.  <BR>When I bought my 1984 sc, white smoke would come out of the tailpipes for the first 5 minutes the car idled. If you drove it, then the smoke went away a lot faster.  I know what oil smoke is like, I had a 1978 Honda accord with bad vavle guide seals.  This smoke dissapeted quickly and had no burnt oil smell.  The engine consumed no oil but leaked coolant at the thermostat housing.  The  hoses all look swolen. I'm replacing them now.<BR>As I took the head off the car, I found the intake manifold was bolted on at zero torque.  Water was leaking from it into the intake manifold and tot he outside world.  The exhauast manifold was held on with finger tight bolts.    <BR>I suspected that water was entering the cylinders from day one, so I had all of the parts on the shelf already for a head gasket swap.  <BR>To defend myself for suggesting copper gasket sealant on the head gasket, it's permatex and the says "recommended for head gaskets" right on the side of it in bold print.  And, Chilton's suggests using the copper gasket sealant for the head bolt threads.  I have a brand new set of head bolts too.  I will purchase a degree torque socket before I torque the bolts.  I remember how tight they were when I removed the old bolts! <BR>Here's a question:  Why was there no gasket between the exhaust manifold head sheild and the head on a post-recall 84-85 2.5L duke?.  My Fel Pro gasket set came with only one exhaust manifold gasket so I admit I am confused by this.  When I look at the heat shield, it's clear that it does not seal against the head without a gasket as there is a black residude leak pattern on it.  I don't want any exhaust leaks!  <BR>Ogre, Fieroshop, Fierodave, and any other experts out there:  Why is there only one exhaust manifold gasket?  What would happen if I put one between the head and sheild and another between the shield and exhaust manifold?<BR>Once I have completed the test for warpage on the head, it's all going back together.  <BR>It's been a whole day since I last drove it . . .<P>[This message has been edited by LuckyTheyWereEverMade (edited 01-15-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by LuckyTheyWereEverMade (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-15-2001||01:44 PM||||Yes. head bolts get scrapped each time the head is off.<P>Like 88 said way up by the top... you can do passage cleanup as long as you don't hit the seats. If you nick even one seat your hosed. Don't do a full port match on your first try at this work. That level of porting is as much art as science. No written document can give you all the details. If you screw it up you can hurt performance. Doing a basic cleanup is unlikely to hurt anything as long as you are carefull.<P>The recall does not call for a gasket between the shield and head. The sheild should seal unless the mainfold flange is messed up or there is some other problem. If you do put a gasket here you will have to modify it. the normal manifold gasket will not work as is. I doubt adding a gasket there would hurt anything. It's just going to be a pain to install.<P>BTW... you will notice the bottom of the exhaust manifold gasket has notched holes. Put the up shield and manifold then insert the bottom bolts about a 1/4" into the block. Then drop the gasket between the manifold and shield. <P>If you cut appart another gasket for between the shield and block you would then put a section in behind the shield and stick in a top bolt to keep it from falling. You will probably need forceps or long thin needle nose pliers to get it in place.<P>I haven't seen that particular copper sealant yet. You would use it the same way. only around the water jacket and maybe oil passages and then only sparingly. You don't coat the whole thing. Do not do this if you have FelPro PrintOSeal or similar head gasket. That gasket already has silcone sealant printed on it from the factory.<P>It doesn't matter to much which sealer you put on the bolts as long as they are all sealed. On the ones that don't need sealer make sure you film the threads with oil so all bolts torque the same. If you have some dry and some lubed with sealer you won't get even torque no mater what the wrench says. A film of oil on the bottom of the bolt heads helps too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-15-2001||10:06 PM||||Thanks for the valuable advice, TheOgre!  An old friend of mine said he had cleaned up the intake and exhaust passages without doing a valve job and that it was no big deal.  It's good to hear that if I am careful there should be no problem.  <P>I will remove the burrs and quit while I am ahead (no pun intended here).  I should start to reassemble it tonight, but I still have some cleaning to do first.  I am looking forward to full compression.  I have been told the little duke can get 30 mpg on the highway.  I'd love to see it!<P>Any tips for removing the exhaust manifold to exhaust pipe gasket.  It is a metal ring with a conical leading edge.  I don't know how to get the old one off.  It seems like a magic wand would be the right tool for this one.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||01-15-2001||11:41 PM||||The ones I've seen are all a wire mesh filled with some type of graphite compound. You usually can just pull them off. I've seen some ceramic ones but never on a FWD type engine. <P>I've gotten 32 hwy from my 87 4 on a long trip. 27 isn't uncommon.||||reg||1
